#!/usr/bin/env bash
#
# radicle-seed-prune - automatically detect and prune lower-value repos, spam and abuse from
# a Radicle (heartwood) node's storage. Two jobs at once: it reclaims disk from stale giants,
# abandoned repos and disposable ones, and it moderates mass-generated spam, media dumps and
# peers who push their own files into repos they are not a delegate of.
#
# DRY-RUN by default: it only prints the plan and changes nothing. Pass --apply to act.
#
# WHAT "PRUNE" MEANS
#   There is no `rad rm`. Unseeding or blocking a repo frees ZERO bytes, since those only
#   change policy. Disk is reclaimed solely by deleting the repo's storage directory.
#   So pruning a repo is three steps, in this order:
#       rad unseed <rid>   # drop any existing seeding policy
#       rad block  <rid>   # set an explicit block so the default allow-all won't refetch
#       rm -rf storage/<rid>
#   Order matters: `rad unseed` removes whichever single policy row a repo has, so it
#   must run BEFORE `rad block`, never after, or it would wipe the block we just set.
#
# RECOVERABILITY
#   A pruned repo waits QUARANTINE_DAYS in AUDIT_DIR/quarantine, where one command puts it
#   back ("quarantine restore <rid>"). After that it is re-fetchable from the network
#   (rad unseed <rid>, then rad seed <rid>) AS LONG AS other nodes still hold it. Both
#   commands: rad seed on its own rewrites only an existing policy row's scope, so on a
#   blocked repo it prints success and leaves the block standing. That re-fetch is why the
#   size/age rules keep a minimum other-seed count. Rule A's junk-id and the verdicts of
#   rules D, F and H ship with a floor of 0 and may prune the last copy the network is known
#   to hold, which is what the quarantine is for. Every prune is recorded in an audit log.
#
# SELECTION  (a repo is pruned if it is denied, see below, or if it is NOT excluded AND matches
# a rule; rule G is about peers instead, and prunes nothing)
#
#   Exclusions (never touched by a rule):
#     - pinned repos        (config web.pinned.repositories)
#     - private repos       (rad ls --private, or the identity document says private)
#     - our own repos       (rad ls, or this node has signed refs in the repo)
#     - freshly written     (storage dir mtime within FRESH_GUARD_DAYS; in-flight fetch)
#     - unknown age         (no readable refs; left alone out of caution, and counted
#                            against MAX_SCAN_FAIL_PCT: a whole storage of it is a
#                            broken scan, not an exclusion)
#     - kept                (one rid per line in AUDIT_DIR/keep.txt; a verdict a human has
#                            already overruled, written there by "quarantine restore". A
#                            did:key: line there clears an identity of rule H, and keeps none
#                            of its repos from the other rules)
#     - pardoned            (UNDO_PARDON: a repo rad-prune is known to have pruned as media by
#                            mistake, until the release named beside it. Spared the media
#                            rule (F) alone)
#
#   Denied (pruned and blocked on sight, before any rule; pinned, private, own and kept
#   still win, the other exclusions do not):
#     - a repo id in AUDIT_DIR/deny.txt, blocked ahead of time when it is not in storage yet
#     - every repo whose DELEGATES include an identity in deny.txt, which is blocked too. A
#       listed identity's push into someone else's repo does not count, or listing it would
#       let it condemn any repo it can comment on.
#     - every repo whose delegates' branches and tags, history included, hold copies of the
#       files listed in AUDIT_DIR/deny-files.tsv: at least COPY_MIN_BYTES of them, making up at
#       least COPY_MIN_PCT% of the bytes there. A file some repo held before every repo it is
#       listed from counts for none. This one is the tool's inference from a
#       person's verdict, so the runaway caps and the ratchet count it like a rule's.
#
#   Rule A - junk-named & abandoned, in two branches that differ in how sure we are:
#     A/junk-name: name contains a disposable WORD (test, tmp, old, demo, ...)
#       AND no new commit/COB for > JUNK_STALE_DAYS
#       AND other-seeds >= JUNK_MIN_SEEDS   (default 1: never prune the last copy on a guess)
#       AND its history starts no more than IMPORT_SPARE_DAYS before its `rad init`
#           (a repo whose commit dates do not read counts as not imported, with a WARN)
#     A/junk-id:   the name is NOTHING BUT a random hex id (08a25d0f666d)
#       AND no new commit/COB for > JUNK_STALE_DAYS
#       AND other-seeds >= JUNK_ID_MIN_SEEDS  (default 0: may prune the last copy we know of)
#
#   Rule B - size outlier, stale, replicated:
#       size > ABS_SIZE_FLOOR_MB           (absolute outlier)
#       AND size >= the REL_PCTL-th percentile of all repo sizes  (relative outlier)
#       AND no new commit/COB for > OUTLIER_STALE_DAYS
#       AND other-seeds >= MIN_OTHER_SEEDS
#
#   "Other-seeds" is how many OTHER nodes our routing table says announce the repo. It
#   is NOT proof a copy exists elsewhere (gossip is incomplete, and the author almost
#   certainly still has theirs), so a floor of 0 is licence to prune the last copy WE
#   KNOW OF. junk-id, spam-batch, the three media verdicts and the malware-op verdict get
#   it, on the reasoning that for machine-generated bulk, for a pile of media with no project
#   around it, and for a malware toolkit, "nobody else seeds it" measures worthlessness rather
#   than rarity. Rules
#   B and C keep a floor: a big or long-abandoned UNIQUE repo is the very thing a seed
#   exists to preserve.
#
#   Rule C - long-abandoned, replicated (catch-all for whatever the other rules missed):
#       no new commit/COB for > STALE_YEARS_DAYS
#       AND other-seeds >= MIN_OTHER_SEEDS
#
#   Rules B and C prune only under disk pressure, while free space is under the relaxed
#   threshold (see DISK_AWARE below).
#
#   A SPAM BATCH is a set of repos one script stamped out from a single template: the same name
#   shape with a slot filled in, the same description with a number swapped.
#
#   Rule D - spam batches, decided by the CORPUS, not by one repo:
#       name skeleton contains a random-id slot   (SPAM_REQUIRE_ID=0 accepts any varying slot)
#       AND >= SPAM_MIN_BATCH repos share that name skeleton
#       AND >= SPAM_DESC_AGREE_PCT% of them share ONE description skeleton
#       AND the repo is older than SPAM_STALE_DAYS
#       AND other-seeds >= SPAM_MIN_SEEDS  (default 0: may prune the last copy we know of)
#
#   Names and descriptions are skeletonised (digit runs become #, random-id tokens become %)
#   and rule D looks for a template that repeats. TWO INDEPENDENT SIGNALS are required, the
#   name shape AND the description, because a bulk mirror import shares a name skeleton while
#   each of its repos carries its own real description. Timing is not a signal: a bulk import
#   lands its repos together too, and commit dates are the pusher's to set.
#
#   A MEDIA DUMP is a repo whose tracked files are video, images or audio with no project
#   around them. Radicle storage is for collaborating on code; a repo holding one .mp4 and
#   nothing else is using the seed as free file hosting, and that is what the spam wave does.
#
#   Rule F - media dumps, decided by the repo's OWN content:
#       >= MEDIA_MIN_BYTES of media files on the refs this repo's own delegates control
#       AND everything that is NOT media adds up to < MEDIA_TEXT_MAX_BYTES,
#           OR (the ratio path, verdict media-ratio) to < MEDIA_RATIO_TEXT_PER_MILLE/1000 of
#           the images, video and audio at the tips of its branches and tags, which come to
#           >= MEDIA_RATIO_MIN_BYTES, while the repo holds no archive and the tips hold
#           nothing besides the media but <= MEDIA_RATIO_MAX_FILES READMEs
#       AND no source file and no build file (MEDIA_SOURCE_EXTS, MEDIA_BUILD_NAMES)
#       AND the repo is older than MEDIA_STALE_DAYS
#       AND other-seeds >= MEDIA_MIN_SEEDS  (default 0: may prune the last copy we know of)
#     A repo whose name starts like a hostname, holding < MEDIA_HOST_MAX_BYTES of media, is a
#     seed's logo and is spared by every path, unless that hostname ends in a media extension.
#
#   Rule F, batch path - the same dump published again and again, decided by the CORPUS:
#       >= MEDIA_MIN_BYTES of media, of which >= MEDIA_MIN_BYTES sits in blobs that
#         MEDIA_MIN_BATCH or more repos also hold, each with < MEDIA_TEXT_CEIL_BYTES of
#         anything else, and that this repo was not the first to hold
#       AND everything that is NOT media adds up to < MEDIA_TEXT_CEIL_BYTES
#       AND no build file; a source file does not spare it
#       AND the same age and seed checks
#
#   Rule G - parasite peers. Judges a PEER and prunes nothing. Blocking one takes --block-peers
#     and an answer, either a y at the prompt or --yes standing in for it:
#       one file of theirs, byte for byte, in >= PARASITE_MIN_REPOS repos they do not delegate
#       AND >= PARASITE_MIN_BYTES of media across those repos
#       AND < PARASITE_TEXT_MAX_BYTES of anything else, anywhere
#       AND they are not a delegate of ANY repo in storage, and are not us
#
#   Rule H - malware operations. Judges an IDENTITY, then single repos. By default it prunes
#     and blocks nothing: whoever runs the seed and agrees adds them to the deny list. With
#     MALWARE_PRUNE=1 it prunes every repo a named identity is a delegate of (malware-op), and
#     an --apply run with --block-peers blocks each identity whose repos it prunes. A repo
#     named on its own is only reported. A repo an identity this rule leaves out signed is
#     spared, and a named delegate of a spared repo is not blocked. Over the names and
#     descriptions of the repos an identity is a delegate of and signed refs in, leaving out
#     identities that delegate a pinned, kept or our own repo, or are in the keep list or the
#     deny list:
#       one of MALWARE_STRONG_WORDS
#       AND >= MALWARE_MIN_WORDS distinct words of MALWARE_STRONG_WORDS and MALWARE_WEAK_WORDS
#       AND >= MALWARE_MIN_REPOS repos, and >= MALWARE_MIN_SHARE_PCT% of its repos, holding any
#     where a repo holding a word counts only for the identity whose key signed the commit
#     that created it
#     Then one repo the identity path did not name, that is not pinned, private, kept, our
#     own or on the deny list, and none of whose signers that review leaves out:
#       one of MALWARE_STRONG_WORDS in its name or description
#       AND one in a path at a tip of its branches and tags, or in a commit subject on them
#       AND its first commit no more than MALWARE_ORIGINAL_DAYS before its `rad init`
#
#   THE TEXT BUDGET is what tells a dump from a real repo that happens to carry media: a game
#   or a documented photo archive has a README, a licence or a build file and clears
#   MEDIA_TEXT_MAX_BYTES many times over. Bytes rather than file count, or one 40-byte
#   placeholder would buy an exemption. A source or build file spares a repo from the dump and
#   ratio paths whatever its size, as a 2 KB README spares it from the dump path; all three
#   are exemptions anyone can buy. On the batch path, where a README under
#   MEDIA_TEXT_CEIL_BYTES no longer spares, a build file still does: a small project forked a
#   few times shares its assets, and pruning it costs more than missing a campaign that learns
#   to add an empty Makefile. A source file does not spare there, because leak campaigns have
#   shipped scripts beside their media.
#
#   WHAT A FILE IS, NOT WHAT IT IS CALLED. Extensions are only a fast path. A file on neither
#   list is read (its first 16 bytes) and matched against media signatures, so renaming a
#   video to .dat does not hide it, and compressed data (a .csv.gz) is judged by what it
#   unpacks to. A file matching nothing counts as text, so an unfamiliar one spares the repo
#   rather than condemning it. A recognised TEXT file over MEDIA_SNIFF_TEXT_BYTES is read too,
#   or a video called README.md would both hide itself and blow the very budget that spares
#   the repo.
#
#   ONLY THE REPO'S OWN CONTENT COUNTS: the canonical branches and tags, plus the namespaces of
#   the delegates named in refs/rad/id, a ref the local node writes only after verifying the
#   identity document's signatures. The delegates' own issues and comments therefore count,
#   because a dump hides as well in an attachment as in a commit. A COB is read across its
#   whole history, since it gains one commit per op and each tree holds only that op's own
#   files, so an attachment from an older comment is still on disk. Only the ops a delegate
#   signed count: that history also reaches every other commenter's ops, and the commits of
#   any patch a delegate replied to. openssl checks each signature. Every OTHER peer's
#   namespace is skipped, so pushing a video onto a stranger's near-empty repo cannot put THEIR
#   repo in the plan. Skipping beats subtracting what those namespaces hold: replication
#   mirrors a repo's own branches and COBs into every peer that seeds it, so subtracting what
#   those peers hold would erase the repo's own README and issue payloads and leave a real
#   project looking like a dump. What the delegates hold is this repo's content whoever else
#   has a copy. A repo we cannot read is left out of rule F, as is one over MEDIA_MAX_REFS
#   refs or MEDIA_MAX_OPS issue and patch ops: walking them all costs minutes to learn that a
#   widely replicated repo is not a dump.
#   So is one whose listing dies part-way, since the fragment reads as a repo holding less
#   text, and less text is what condemns. A branch or tag is read at its TIP, so media
#   committed and then deleted is missed; walking every branch's history would cost far more
#   and would count against a repo that legitimately dropped a big asset years ago. The walk
#   stops as soon as the repo has more text than any verdict allows, which on a mirror of a
#   real project is the first few files out of a million.
#
#   A TOKEN README defeats the budget, and below a few MB of media nothing measurable about ONE
#   repo tells it from the README of a small real project: they are the same bytes. Above that,
#   the ratio path catches a README and nothing else beside a great deal of media; a project
#   carries a licence, a script or a second document. For the rest there is the batch path.
#   Publishing the same files across many repos is evidence no single repo can manufacture, so
#   where it exists the budget widens to MEDIA_TEXT_CEIL_BYTES and the fig leaf stops working.
#   The FIRST holder is left out of its own batch, or reposting somebody's photos into five
#   repos of your own would put their repo in the plan instead of yours.
#
#   A DUMP NO OTHER NODE SEEDS is pruned like any other: the evidence is what the repo holds,
#   and a dump nobody else seeds is still a dump. MEDIA_MIN_SEEDS=1 keeps that copy, and rule F
#   then LISTS what the floor spared rather than passing over it in silence, because a floor
#   nobody sees is a standing exemption for anyone who makes sure nobody else seeds their dump.
#
# RULE G JUDGES A PEER, NOT A REPO, and nothing it finds is ever deleted or pruned. A peer
#   who pushes into repos it is not a delegate of is normally a contributor, so the only
#   evidence that counts here is ONE FILE, byte for byte, appearing in PARASITE_MIN_REPOS
#   repos where that peer is not a delegate and where the repo's own refs do not hold it. A
#   person attaching a screenshot to an issue posts a different file each time; the same
#   object id turning up in unrelated repos is a campaign. Totals alone are not enough:
#   someone active across many repos accumulates megabytes honestly.
#
#   A DELEGATE IS NEVER ACCUSED, wherever in storage they are one. The node runs delegate refs
#   through the same block list before it checks the signature threshold, so blocking a
#   delegate stops the repos they are a delegate of from replicating properly.
#
#   THE ACT IS SEPARATE FROM THE FINDING, and takes two opt-ins. A block is permanent, applies
#   to every repo at once, and the person blocked is never told, so --block-peers asks for each
#   peer, and refuses outright when there is no terminal and no --yes to stand in for the
#   answers. --block-peers --yes is the unattended form, and blocks every peer the rule names.
#   It is also an action of its own and does not imply --apply, since nobody should have to
#   delete five hundred repos to deal with one peer. The plan prints the exact `rad block`
#   line for every peer it names whether or not that flag was given, so an operator can act on
#   one peer without handing the tool a standing licence over the whole list, and each block is
#   written to the audit log with the evidence behind it. Blocking is what makes it stick: the
#   node filters every namespaced ref it receives through the block list whatever its seeding
#   scope is, so refs dropped here do not come back. Dropping them frees no disk until git gc
#   runs, which this tool does not do.
#
#   HOW OLD A REPO IS, for rules D and F, is the older of two dates: the oldest date on any
#   of its refs, and the day this seed first saw it (FIRST_SEEN). Ref dates are set by
#   whoever pushed them, so a spammer who rewrites every ref gets a fresh one; the ledger
#   cannot be reached from outside this seed. Rules A, B and C are about abandonment instead,
#   so they use activity:
#
#   "Activity" = ANY signed change, however small. Every Radicle interaction (a commit,
#   a new issue/patch, a comment, a reaction, an edit, a label) is a git commit appended
#   under some peer's refs/cobs/*, and it also advances that peer's refs/rad/sigrefs. The
#   tool reads the newest creatordate across ALL refs (every namespace included), so the
#   freshest of any of these wins. creatordate is when the change was authored, not when we
#   replicated it, so a just-fetched old comment correctly still reads as old.
#
# SAFETY
#   A pruned repo is moved to AUDIT_DIR/quarantine and deleted for real QUARANTINE_DAYS later,
#   so a wrong verdict has a week in which one command undoes it. The node was told to unseed
#   and block every repo in there before it moved, so nothing on the node points at the
#   quarantine: rm -rf on it is safe at any moment, and costs only the ability to restore.
#   Per-run runaway caps abort a suspiciously large plan unless --force. The node is left
#   running; deleting a blocked repo's dir live is safe. After a large first run, one
#   `sudo systemctl restart radicle-node` clears the stale "inventory announce limit" log
#   warning (optional; --restart-node does it for you if run with the rights to). On some
#   heartwood versions `rad node inventory` may still list the removed RIDs afterwards; that
#   is cosmetic, the repos are gone from disk and blocked from re-seeding.
#
# USAGE
#   Installed on PATH as rad-prune, `rad` runs this as one of its own subcommands, which is
#   the spelling below. rad-prune and ./rad-prune take the same arguments.
#
#   rad prune                        # preview (dry-run), print the plan
#   rad prune --apply                # apply; asks [y/N] first when run in a terminal
#   rad prune --apply --yes          # answer that prompt with y (scripts / cron)
#   rad prune --apply --force        # apply even if the plan trips the runaway caps
#   rad prune --apply --restart-node # ...and restart the node service afterwards
#   rad prune --block-peers          # block the peers the rules name, asking per peer
#   rad prune --block-peers --yes    # ...without asking (cron)
#
#   rad prune quarantine list             # what a past run pruned and still holds
#   rad prune quarantine restore <rid>    # put it back in storage and re-seed it
#   rad prune quarantine delete <rid>     # or --all: delete now, for good
#   rad prune quarantine purge            # delete whatever is past its window
#   rad prune quarantine files <rid>      # its images, video, audio and archives, as rows
#                                         # to append to deny-files.tsv
#
# --apply scans ONCE, prints the plan, then in a terminal asks before deleting; run
# non-interactively (cron, a pipe) it just applies. Confirming interactively also bypasses the
# runaway caps. --yes (-y) answers every prompt a run asks, the per-peer block prompt
# included: --block-peers --yes is how a cron job blocks the peers rule G finds, and with
# --apply and MALWARE_PRUNE=1 the identities rule H names, while --block-peers alone with
# nobody to ask (cron, a pipe) blocks nobody and prints the rad commands instead.
# --block-peers prunes nothing on its own; pass --apply too to do both.
#
# ENVIRONMENT
#   Everything is configured by environment variable, and there are no tuning flags. RAD_HOME
#   is the same variable heartwood itself reads, so this reads like any other rad invocation:
#
#     RAD_HOME=/var/lib/radicle rad prune              # a seed home that is not ours
#     RAD=/nix/store/.../bin/rad RAD_HOME=/var/lib/radicle rad-prune
#     JOBS=4 OUTLIER_STALE_DAYS=120 rad prune          # any tunable in the config block
#
#   RAD (default: rad from PATH) and RAD_HOME (default: `rad path`, else ~/.radicle) locate the
#   node; STORAGE, CONFIG and AUDIT_DIR derive from RAD_HOME. Every threshold in the config
#   block is overridable the same way. A handful of values there are plain constants instead,
#   the ones you would have to read the implementation to choose a number for.
#
# Run as the user that owns the Radicle home (the seed account), or point RAD_HOME at it.
# Cron-safe: dry-run by default, deterministic, and self-logging.
